# G094 — Log File Maker

*v2. See `log-file-maker.md` for prose: append-only discipline,
level-filter at write time, rotation as ring-with-archive, tab-
delimited format for `grep|awk|sort` interop, query results preserve
insertion order.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Observability

[vault-observability
    IN ORDER TO
        funnel every event into a single logger
        answer operator queries about last-hour errors
        and rotate periodically into dated archive files

    @logger <- [log[new {threshold: "INFO", max-entries: 10000}]]

    sync @any-events {
        event @ts @level @category @message ->
            [log[log {logger: @logger,
                       ts: @ts, level: @level,
                       category: @category, message: @message}]]

        last-hour-errors ->
            @now    <- [clock[now]]
            @range  <- [log[time-range
                              {logger: @logger,
                               from: @now - 3600000, to: @now}]]
            @errors <- [log[level
                              {logger: @logger, min-level: "ERROR"}]]
            @hits   <- @range intersected-with @errors
            [ui[render-log-view {entries: @hits}]]

        rotate-to-archive @date ->
            [vault[append
                    {path: "observability/app.log." + @date,
                     content: [log[to-text {logger: @logger}]]}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[log
    new                  {threshold, max-entries}
    log                  {logger, ts, level, category, message}
    level                {logger, min-level}
    category             {logger, category}
    message-contains     {logger, needle}
    time-range           {logger, from, to}
    to-text              {logger}
    parse-entries        {text}                    ;; || malformed-line
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: write-time threshold as `where` gate

[log[log {logger: @l, ts: @ts, level: @lvl, ...}]
    @entry
        where level at least @l:level-threshold
            -> append to @l:active buffer
               (if @l:active overflows @l:max-entries,
                oldest moves to @l:archive, never back)
        otherwise
            -> discard (never enters the buffer)
]

Filtered-out entries never enter memory — the policy is "capture at
the point it matters." Production systems running with INFO threshold
pay zero cost for the DEBUG entries the app emits.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: two-tier storage `where` recency decides

[log[query]
    scans @l:active first
    IN ORDER TO
        answer most operator queries from the fast hot tier
        and fall back to @l:archive only for compliance / historical reads

    queries return results in insertion order
        (chronology is a contract; time flows forward)
]

The active buffer is bounded and fast; the archive grows and is
scanned rarely. Same two-tier shape as every real log system
(log4j, systemd-journald, logrotate).

---

## Demo

[demo
    @l <- [log[new {threshold: "DEBUG", max-entries: 100}]]
    [log[log {logger: @l, ts: 100, level: "INFO",
               category: "boot", message: "starting up"}]]
    [log[log {logger: @l, ts: 300, level: "WARN",
               category: "net", message: "slow response"}]]
    [log[log {logger: @l, ts: 400, level: "ERROR",
               category: "db", message: "connection lost"}]]

    -> [log[level {logger: @l, min-level: "WARN"}]]
    ;; -> [300/WARN/net/..., 400/ERROR/db/...]

    -> [log[category {logger: @l, category: "db"}]]
    ;; -> [400/ERROR/db/connection lost]
]
